# Logitech G515 TKL keyboards on their cable, vendor HID++ interface ->
# uaccess for the seat user: the wired-only G515 TKL (046d:c358) and the
# G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL (046d:c355), and the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL's node of its
# own behind its receiver, at the end of this file. The ids are from Logitech
# G HUB's device database. The wired G515 TKL was read off the hardware on
# 2026-10-17; the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL has not been.
#
# Interface 02: on the wired G515 TKL it holds the vendor collection (usage
# page 0xFF00) alone, as on the G915. A keyboard's other interfaces carry its
# keystrokes, and read access there would be a keylogger surface. For a G515
# LIGHTSPEED TKL on its cable, gearbench-ctl report gives each node's
# interface and whether its report descriptor holds HID++, read from sysfs,
# so it needs no access to the node. On the cable Gearbench opens interface
# 02 alone, so if HID++ is on another, put a copy of this file in
# /etc/udev/rules.d/ without the c355 line: it overrides this file by name,
# and a reinstall or upgrade leaves it alone. Then run sudo udevadm control
# --reload, replug the keyboard, and paste the report's block into a device
# report.
#
# Interface 02 also carries the modifier keys held (Ctrl, Shift, Alt, Super
# and FN, each press and release) once a host writes 0x8051 fn 3 `03 ff`, as
# Gearbench does at each setup to light the FN keys. The wired G515 TKL keeps
# sending them until a replug or a power cycle (G HUB's traffic with it,
# captured 2026-10-21). The G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL is taken to keep them through
# a replug of its cable, on its battery, until it is switched off, as the
# G915 X LIGHTSPEED did (70-g915-x.rules). Gearbench reads FN alone. Any
# program of the seat's user can write that and read them, and one that holds
# the node open reads another account's after a user switch. On a machine
# several people use, the group grant in 70-gearbench-uinput.rules, in a
# copy of this file in /etc/udev/rules.d/, limits the node to the group's
# accounts. A member then reads it from any session, whoever has the seat.
#
# Matched on properties, as 70-g915.rules is, for the reason it gives. The
# G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL over Bluetooth has no line here and is not driven.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c358", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c355", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"

# The G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL through its LIGHTSPEED receiver (046d:c547): the
# hidraw node of its own, 0003:046D:40B4.<n>, that the kernel's
# hid-logitech-dj gives a device paired to a c547, as it gives the G502 X
# PLUS its 4099 node (70-g502x.rules). Not yet checked on this hardware: the
# 40b4 id and the receiver are from Solaar 1.1.13's listing of the keyboard
# and G HUB's device and receiver records, read 2026-10-17 and 2026-10-18.
#
# This node carries what is typed on the keyboard as well as its HID++
# traffic: hid-logitech-dj marks a keyboard on a LIGHTSPEED link as taking
# keyboard reports, gives its node a keyboard collection
# (kbd_lightspeed_1_3_descriptor on a c547) and hands it the receiver's
# keystrokes (read from the kernel's source on 2026-10-18, not checked on
# hardware). So with this line installed, any program the user of the
# active seat session runs can read what is typed on the G515, not only
# Gearbench, which drops those reports unread. The owner granted it on
# 2026-10-18 ("Grant, with a warning"), as the G915 TKL's node of its own
# behind its receiver was granted. It carries no other device's reports
# while nothing else is paired to the receiver (70-g502x.rules says why).
#
# The grant crosses accounts, as 70-gearbench-uinput.rules says of
# /dev/uinput's: a program that opened the node keeps reading it after another
# account gets the seat, and so reads what that account types on the G515, a
# password at the login screen included. On a machine several people use, that
# file's group grant, in a copy of this file in /etc/udev/rules.d/, limits the
# node to the group's accounts. A member then reads it from any session,
# whoever has the seat.
#
# Matched by the HID device's name, as 70-g502x.rules matches the mouse: udev
# gives this node the receiver's properties, so a property match would grant
# the receiver's own interfaces too. KERNELS matches a parent's kernel name,
# which for the paired device is bus:vendor:product.instance.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:046D:40B4.*", TAG+="uaccess"
